I agree with that, but I think making that change would make them more
balancing for spoiled players and not affect unspoiled players as
much.

As for wishes in general, we can assume that most spoiled players know
what to wish for and most unspoiled players have no clue what to wish
for or maybe even what they /can/ wish for.  It's only a guess, but I
think most early wishes for all players come from fountains and magic
lamps.  These are both (fairly easily) discoverable options for
unspoiled players.  WoW are so rare that decreasing their maximum
charges when randomly found only eliminates the unbalancing nature for
the spoiled player while not changing much for the unspoiled player,
who probably wouldn't know what to wish for in the first place.

For the spoiled player who makes it to the castle, fewer wishes means
a more varied ascension kit and more hard decisions that need to be
made (which I consider a Good Thing).  For the unspoiled player who
makes it to the castle (I'm just conjecturing here), they probably
have a good idea of what to wish for as well and are probably in the
same boat as the spoiled player.

